WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:39.220
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. And in our show today, we have two stories that have a lot in common, but to explain the many things they have in common right here and now would reveal so many spoilers that I'm going to say very little. Rather than do the kind of introduction I usually do right here, all I'm going to say is that each of these stories centers around some guy, and the guy in each story has the right to remain silent, and he does not exercise that right. Like they say in the movies, he could take the easy way, or he could take the hard way. And each of these guys goes way out of his way to take the hard way, not totally realizing what he's getting into. And let's just get right to it.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是公共广播国际公司分发的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。在我们今天的节目中，有两个有很多共同点的故事，但是如果要在此地此时解释他们众多的共同点，就会剧透太多，所以我决定少说为妙。与其做我通常在这里做的那种开场白，我只想说，这两个故事都围绕着某个家伙展开，而每个故事里的家伙都有保持沉默的权利，但他却没有行使这个权利。就像电影里说的那样，他可以选择一条轻松的路，或者选择一条艰难的路。而这两个家伙都费尽周折地选择走艰难的路，却并没有完全意识到自己卷入了什么。让我们直接切入正题吧。

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:57.390
<v Ira Glass>Act One. First Rule of the Apple Store: Don't Talk About the Apple Store. At some point or another, you've probably known somebody like this, somebody who can't resist the urge to talk back. Not out of principle, but out of sheer chemical instinct. It's built into his personality. That's the guy in this first story. Ben Calhoun's the reporter.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。苹果店的第一条规则：不要谈论苹果店。在某个时候，你可能认识这样的人，那种无法抗拒回嘴冲动的人。不是出于原则，而是出于纯粹的化学本能。这是他性格中固有的。这就是第一个故事里的那个家伙。本·卡尔霍恩是记者。

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:14.470
<v Ben Calhoun>The guy in this story, and he'd tell you this himself, he's a sarcastic jerk. He's honest that way. He'll say he has no filter, he has foot in mouth disease, he's a loud mouth, a big mouth. Like, this is how he described getting kicked out of Catholic high school during his sophomore year.
<v Ben Calhoun>这个故事里的家伙——他自己也会这样告诉你——是个尖酸刻薄的混蛋。他在这方面很诚实。他会说自己没有过滤网，管不住嘴，是个大嘴巴，嘴碎。比如，他是这样描述自己在高二时被天主教高中开除的经过的。

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:38.950
<v Joe Lipari>I cursed at a nun. My English teacher was a nun, and one day she's screaming at me. Like, "we all know you're just some little thug hoodlum. And I was like, "we all know you're a [BLEEP] bitch." And they were like, "you can't go to school here anymore." And I thought that was kind of ironic, because they preach forgiveness.
<v Joe Lipari>我骂了一个修女。我的英语老师是个修女，有一天她冲我大喊大叫。就像是，“我们都知道你不过是个小地痞流氓。”而我当时就说：“我们都知道你是个混蛋婊子。”然后他们的反应是：“你不能再来这里上学了。”我觉得这有点讽刺，因为他们宣扬宽容。

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.280
<v Ben Calhoun>So what are some other instances where being like--
<v Ben Calhoun>那么还有哪些其他的例子，比如——

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.620
<v Joe Lipari>Being me got me in trouble?
<v Joe Lipari>做我自己给我惹了麻烦？

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.670
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, well just like-- mouthing off got you in trouble.
<v Ben Calhoun>对，好吧，就像是——顶嘴给你惹了麻烦。

00:01:46.670 --> 00:02:05.060
<v Joe Lipari>Every day. I got tons from the army. I got tons from high schools. I got tons from my current school. I got-- relationships, my parents. I don't know, I'm mouthy.
<v Joe Lipari>天天如此。我从军队那儿惹了一堆麻烦。我从高中那儿惹了一堆麻烦。我从现在的学校惹了一堆麻烦。我有——人际关系，我的父母。我不知道，我这张嘴就是碎。

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:43.350
<v Ben Calhoun>The mouthy guy is a guy named Joe Lipari. And I should probably say, this part of Joe's personality-- it doesn't always play badly for Joe. Like when he was in the army, Joe talked back to a drill sergeant who was telling him to cut off his sideburns. Joe recited the military regulations on sideburn length from memory, which made the sergeant mad, but won over his battalion commander, who became a friend and mentor for Joe. Joe also does stand-up comedy, where mouthiness is encouraged. But those things, the good things-- that's not why we're here. This thing that happened to Joe, it started in September of 2009 when Joe needed a new cell phone.
<v Ben Calhoun>这个嘴碎的家伙名叫乔·利帕里。我可能应该说明一下，乔性格中的这一部分——对乔来说并不总是带来坏结果。比如在军队时，乔顶撞了一个叫他把鬓角剃掉的 drill sergeant（训练中士）。乔凭记忆背出了军队关于鬓角长度的规章制度，这让中士很生气，但却赢得了营长的赏识，营长后来成了乔的朋友和导师。乔还表演单口喜剧，在那种地方，嘴碎是受到鼓励的。但那些事情，那些好事——不是我们今天来这的原因。发生在乔身上的这件事，始于2009年9月，当时乔需要一部新手机。

00:02:43.350 --> 00:03:02.250
<v Joe Lipari>I switched to the iPhone with all my friends. We thought we were cool. And mine just never worked. It would freeze, it would shut off, I would charge it and then I would take it off the charger and immediately the battery would be empty of power. And it would just do all the things a computer should not do.
<v Joe Lipari>我和所有的朋友一起换了iPhone。我们觉得自己很酷。而我的手机就是一直不好用。它会死机，会关机，我给它充电，然后拔掉充电器，电池马上就没电了。它会做出所有电脑不该做的事情。

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:17.310
<v Ben Calhoun>So Joe made an appointment and went to the Apple Store. The staff did something they said should fixed the problem. And if it didn't, they said, look, just bring it back and we'll give you a totally new phone. The next day, the phone crashed on him again.
<v Ben Calhoun>于是乔预约了一下，去了苹果店。店里的工作人员做了一些他们说应该能解决问题的事情。如果解决不了，他们说，听着，拿回来，我们给你换个全新的手机。第二天，他的手机又崩溃了。

00:03:17.310 --> 00:04:11.760
<v Joe Lipari>So right after work, I just go to the Apple Store. I get down there and that place is a zoo. It's a really pretty zoo. And so you get in there, and walk up to the concierge that checks your in and stuff at the Apple Store, the guy in the teal shirt. And any time I go up to any employee at a store I always-- because I've worked in retail many times growing up and whatever else-- I'm always very nice, like, hey how's it going? How you doing? Having a good day? And he's like, what can I do for you? I was like, I was here yesterday. My phone's broken. They told me if I come back with it broken again, they'll just switch it out and give me a new phone. The guy said, do you have an appointment? I said, no, I was here yesterday. They gave me the impression that-- just come and it's good to go. And he said, well, we're kind of busy. It's going to be probably about a two hour wait. I was like, cool. I have a book. I'm going to go sit over here.
<v Joe Lipari>所以在下班后，我直接去了苹果店。我到了那里，发现那个地方简直是个动物园。一个非常漂亮的动物园。你走进那里，走向在苹果店负责接待和登记之类的礼宾员，那个穿青色衬衫的家伙。每当我走到店里任何一个员工面前时，我总是——因为我长大的过程中做过很多次零售工作以及其他杂七杂八的工作——我总是非常友善，比如，嘿，最近怎么样？你过得好吗？今天过得愉快吗？然后他说，我能为你做点什么？我说，我昨天来过。我的手机坏了。他们告诉我，如果我带着坏手机再来，他们就会直接给我换一部新手机。那家伙说，你有预约吗？我说，没有，我昨天来过。他们给我的印象是——直接来就行了，随时可以。他说，嗯，我们这会儿挺忙的。可能要等大概两个小时。我说，太酷了。我带了本书。我要去那边坐着。

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:19.030
<v Ben Calhoun>So 30 minutes go by. 45 minutes. An hour. Finally, 90 minutes.
<v Ben Calhoun>于是30分钟过去了。45分钟。一个小时。最后，90分钟。

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:47.530
<v Joe Lipari>90 minutes and I'm like, they've got to be calling me soon. And I just went up and was like, look it's been about two hours. Are we getting any closer to me getting up there? So he looks me up and he's like, oh, it's probably going to be about a two hour wait. I was like, I've already been here for two hours. There's no way I'm waiting for four hours. He was like, well if you had an appointment, this wouldn't happen. That's what appointments are for. You get the appointment, and then you get seen. And I was like, slow your roll. And I shoved something off the table and I stormed out.
<v Joe Lipari>90分钟过去了，我想，他们肯定很快就会叫我了。我就走过去说，听着，已经差不多两个小时了。我轮到上去的进度有进展吗？于是他查了一下我的记录，然后说，哦，可能还要等大概两个小时。我说，我已经在这里等了两个小时了。我绝对不可能再等四个小时。他说，嗯，如果你预约了，就不会发生这种事。预约就是为此设立的。你预约了，然后你就会被接待。我说，放尊重点。我把桌上的东西推掉，气冲冲地走了出去。

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:54.530
<v Ben Calhoun>Joe hopped the subway home to Queens, and the whole way back, even after he got home, he couldn't stop thinking about it.
<v Ben Calhoun>乔坐地铁回到了皇后区，一路上，甚至回到家后，他脑子里一直想着这件事。

00:04:54.530 --> 00:05:11.080
<v Joe Lipari>How can I spend $600 for a thing that does not work? I don't make a lot of money. And so I was kind of steaming, smoking a little pot like you do. It's medicinal, I promise. And I was watching Fight Club. Fight Club was on TV.
<v Joe Lipari>我怎么能花600美元买一个根本不管用的东西？我又没赚很多钱。所以我当时有点在气头上，抽了点大麻，就像你懂的那样。我保证，那是医用的。我当时在看《搏击俱乐部》。电视上正在播《搏击俱乐部》。

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.990
<v Tyler Durden>I want you to hit me as hard as you can.
<v Tyler Durden>我要你狠狠地揍我。

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.920
<v Joe Lipari>And I was just watching Fight Club, chilling out, trying to calm myself down.
<v Joe Lipari>我当时就在看《搏击俱乐部》，放松心情，试图让自己平静下来。

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:31.240
<v Ben Calhoun>So Joe's on his couch-- a big puffy boat of a couch that presumably used to be white. Joe has his laptop. He's distracting himself by looking at Facebook, and like he said, he's watching Fight Club.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以乔正坐在他的沙发上——一个硕大蓬松犹如小船般的沙发，这沙发以前大概是白色的。乔拿着他的笔记本电脑。他在看Facebook来分散注意力，就像他说的，他在看《搏击俱乐部》。

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:44.360
<v Joe Lipari>And the scene from Fight Club comes on where Ed Norton's boss walks in with a piece of paper and he's like, I found this in a copy machine. First rule of Fight Club is-- Second rule of Fight-- like, do you know anything about this?
<v Joe Lipari>然后《搏击俱乐部》里那个场景出现了，爱德华·诺顿的老板拿着一张纸走进来，他说，我在复印机里发现了这个。搏击俱乐部第一条规则是——第二条规则是——你知道这事是怎么回事吗？

00:05:44.360 --> 00:06:18.440
<v Fight Club Narrator>Well, I gotta tell you, I'd be very, very careful who you talk to about that. Because the person who wrote that is dangerous. And this button-down Oxford cloth psycho might just snap and then stalk from office to office within an ArmaLite AR-10 carbine gas-powered semiautomatic weapon pumping round after round into colleagues and coworkers. This might be someone you've known for years. Someone very, very close to you.
<v Fight Club Narrator>好吧，我得告诉你，你得非常非常小心你跟谁谈论这事。因为写下这个的人很危险。而这个穿着衬衫打着领带的牛津布神经病随时可能会突然崩溃，然后拿着一把阿玛莱特AR-10卡宾枪气动半自动武器，在办公室里挨个房间走动，一发又一发地把子弹射进同事们的身体里。这可能是你认识多年的人。一个离你非常非常近的人。

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.640
<v Joe Lipari>I kind of took it and paraphrased it a little bit, and my status was--
<v Joe Lipari>我有点拿过来稍微改写了一下，我的状态是——

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Joe's status on Facebook.
<v Ben Calhoun>乔在Facebook上的状态。

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:39.430
<v Joe Lipari>--and I'm reading it here, Joe Lipari might walk into it Apple Store on Fifth Avenue with an ArmaLite AR-10 gas-powered semiautomatic weapon and pump round after round into one of those smug, fruity little concierges. This may be someone you've known for years, someone very, very close to you.
<v Joe Lipari>——我在这里读一下，乔·利帕里可能会带着一把阿玛莱特AR-10气动半自动武器走进第五大道的苹果店，一发又一发地把子弹射进那些自以为是的、娘娘腔的小礼宾员体内。这可能是你认识多年的人，一个离你非常非常近的人。

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:47.610
<v Ben Calhoun>Just so you know, I did ask Joe about his use of the word fruity here. And he told me that no, it was not homophobic. He was just trying to describe the guy's demeanor.
<v Ben Calhoun>为了以防万一，我确实问过乔这里使用“娘娘腔”（fruity）这个词的意思。他告诉我，不，这不是恐同。他只是想形容那个家伙的举止。

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:52.060
<v Joe Lipari>No, I have a gay cousin. I am the least homophobic person in the world.
<v Joe Lipari>不，我有个同性恋表弟。我是世界上最不恐同的人。

00:06:52.060 --> 00:07:00.720
<v Ben Calhoun>In any case, he types this thing into Facebook and he posts it. And that's that. For like an hour, he just sits there, feet on the coffee table.
<v Ben Calhoun>不管怎样，他把这段话输入到Facebook上并发布了。事情就这样了。大概有一个小时，他就坐在那儿，把脚搁在咖啡桌上。

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:09.100
<v Joe Lipari>And I get a [KNOCKS] 'shave and a haircut, two-bits' knock on the door. And I just think, yay, one of my buddies got in, didn't have to buzz, great.
<v Joe Lipari>然后我听到[敲门声]“刮脸剪头，两美分”式的敲门声。我心想，太棒了，我的某个死党来了，不用按门铃，真棒。

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:12.050
<v Ben Calhoun>Joe jumps up, goes to the front door.
<v Ben Calhoun>乔跳起来，走到前门。

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:31.230
<v Joe Lipari>Yeah, without even looking through the peephole, I swing the door open, kind of go to lean on the door jamb, and that's when I see that there's an officer here, standing right in front of the door, two behind him, and one at the top of the stairs covering the high ground.
<v Joe Lipari>对，甚至都没通过猫眼看一眼，我就猛地把门拉开，身子顺势靠在门框上，就在那时我看到有个警官站在门正前方，后面跟着两个，还有一个在楼梯顶上占据制高点进行掩护。

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:41.320
<v Ben Calhoun>These weren't regular, uniformed cops. They were the guys in street clothes, the guys with black bulletproof vests. One of them had a machine gun.
<v Ben Calhoun>这些可不是普通的、穿着制服的警察。他们是便衣，穿着黑色防弹背心的家伙。其中一个人拿着一把机关枪。

00:07:41.320 --> 00:08:07.690
<v Joe Lipari>All guns drawn, badges out, cliche policemen sunglasses, you know the whole nine yards. And the guy at the door's like, are you Joe Lipari? And I was like, uh, yeah. Well you seem pretty nervous Joe. Well, not every day a guy with an MP5 is knocking on my door. What can I do for you? Is everything OK? And he's like, well you seem to know a lot about guns.
<v Joe Lipari>所有枪都拔出来了，徽章亮出来了，老套的警察太阳镜，你知道的，全套行头。门口的家伙说，你就是乔·利帕里吗？我当时说，呃，是的。好吧，你看起来挺紧张的，乔。废话，又不是每天都有个拿着MP5的家伙敲我的门。我能为你做点什么吗？一切都好吗？然后他说，嗯，你似乎对枪很了解嘛。

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:25.540
<v Ben Calhoun>It's worth stopping here. There was a perfectly good explanation for why Joe could identify the officer's machine gun, something called an MP5. Remember, Joe was in the military. He was a marksman in the army. And he could've said that. He probably should have said it. But of course, he didn't.
<v Ben Calhoun>这里值得停顿一下。对于乔为什么能认出警官的机关枪——一种叫做MP5的武器，有一个非常合理的解释。记住，乔当过兵。他是军队里的神枪手。他本可以这么说。他大概本应该这么说的。但当然，他没有。

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:34.500
<v Joe Lipari>Well, me being a sarcastic jerk, I go, well I watch a lot of Bond movies-- which I giggle, and they don't.
<v Joe Lipari>好吧，由于我是一个尖酸刻薄的混蛋，我说，嗯，我看过很多邦德电影——我咯咯笑了起来，而他们可没笑。

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:47.660
<v Ben Calhoun>What the officers did do then was searched Joe's apartment. And they were asking him all kinds of confusing questions, like about explosives. And Joe, he was trying to figure out why they were there in the first place.
<v Ben Calhoun>警察随后所做的事是搜查了乔的公寓。他们问了他各种让人摸不着头脑的问题，比如关于爆炸物的事。而乔，他当时正试图弄清楚他们为什么会出现在这里。

00:08:47.660 --> 00:09:06.630
<v Joe Lipari>During the week or two before, they had caught some terrorist, or a wannabe terrorist in Queens. So maybe, something in my building, something in the neighborhood, I had no idea. I'm just trying to defuse the situation, make jokes, trying to show these guys-- I'm not trouble. I couldn't be any further from it.
<v Joe Lipari>在此之前的一两周内，他们在皇后区抓获了一些恐怖分子或准恐怖分子。所以也许，我大楼里的某样东西，社区里的某样东西，我完全不知道。我只是在试图缓和局势，开玩笑，试图向这些家伙证明——我不是个麻烦制造者。我离那远着呢。

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:20.510
<v Ben Calhoun>Also remember, while all this is going on, Joe is high. Cops with machine guns are all over his apartment, pulling out drawers and tearing things apart. He has no idea why, and he's totally stoned.
<v Ben Calhoun>还要记住，当这一切发生的时候，乔嗨大了。拿机关枪的警察在他的公寓里到处都是，拉开抽屉，把东西翻得乱七八糟。他根本不知道为什么，而且他完全处于嗑药后的飘飘然状态。

00:09:20.510 --> 00:10:05.510
<v Joe Lipari>The one cop point at the marijuana on the coffee table and kind of snickers. And the other one is like, well, are you a big fan of the reefer? I said, I kind of go through phases if there's a stressful day. And two of them go looking around, and the one stays back with me. And he tries to buddy-buddy with me. He's like, yeah, I understand. I used to be an alcoholic. And in my mind, I'm like, well it's not really the same thing, but all right. And the one cop comes back. And he's like, do you know what an AR-10 is? And I was like, is that why you're here? I was almost dumbfounded-- in my mind, I'm like how in the hell are the cops here because of Facebook? Of all the dumb things I've done in my life. It's a joke. It's a quote from a movie, guy. The movie's on in the other room. I can rewind it, if you like. And then, the one cop was like, well we need to take you downtown for questioning. Homeland security wants to talk to you. I was like, really?
<v Joe Lipari>一个警察指着咖啡桌上的大麻，轻蔑地笑了一下。另一个则说，怎么，你是大麻的超级粉丝吗？我说，如果遇到压力大的一天，我会经历几个阶段。其中两个到处搜查，留了一个在我身边。他试图跟我套近乎。他说，嗯，我理解。我以前是个酒鬼。而在我心里，我觉得，嗯，这其实不是一回事，不过好吧。然后那个警察回来了。他说，你知道什么是AR-10吗？我说，这就是你们来这的原因吗？我简直惊呆了——我心里想，警察怎么可能会因为Facebook而出现在这里？这算是我这辈子做过的所有蠢事中最离谱的了。这只是个玩笑。这是电影里的台词，老兄。那部电影正在另一个房间里播。如果你喜欢，我可以倒带。然后，其中一个警察说，嗯，我们需要把你带到市中心接受讯问。国土安全部想和你谈谈。我说，真的吗？

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:49.450
<v Ben Calhoun>The cops escorted Joe downstairs and he saw that they weren't alone. They'd actually brought back up. There were two extra police cars with officers waiting, in case the guys with the bulletproof vests and the machine gun needed help. They took Joe to a station in Queens and threw him into an interrogation room.
<v Ben Calhoun>警察护送乔下楼，他发现他们并不是孤身一人。他们其实带了增援。有两辆多出来的警车和警察在旁边等着，以防穿防弹背心和拿机关枪的家伙们需要支援。他们把乔带到皇后区的一个警察局，把他扔进了一间审讯室。

00:10:49.450 --> 00:11:13.820
<v Joe Lipari>Like in Batman , where they leave the Joker. It's the same thing. All blank walls, one mirror, which is probably a two-way, crappy table, crappy chairs, and one guy offers you coffee. And I'm in the interrogating room for a good three, three and a half hours. Just different people questioning me, taking turns with me-- good cop/bad cop, bad cop/bad cop, good cop/good cop.
<v Joe Lipari>就像《蝙蝠侠》里把小丑关起来的那样。完全是一回事。全都是白墙，一面单向镜（大概是单向的），一张破桌子，几把破椅子，然后有个人问你要不要咖啡。我在审讯室里呆了足足三到三个半小时。不同的人轮流审讯我，轮番上阵——红脸/白脸，黑脸/黑脸，红脸/红脸。

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:29.470
<v Ben Calhoun>Joe was sure once he explained the situation, they'd see it was a mistake and everything would shake out. It wasn't until four months later that Joe understood he was in serious trouble. I brought a copy of the court records to our interview.
<v Ben Calhoun>乔确信，只要他一解释情况，他们就会看出这是一个误直到四个月后，乔才明白自己惹上了大麻烦。我带了一份法庭记录去我们的采访。

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:34.520
<v Ben Calhoun>Those are the actual charges against you.
<v Ben Calhoun>那些是对你实际的指控。

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:46.353
<v Joe Lipari>A PL240-60 and a PL490-20. The one is make terroristic threats, and the second one is false report of a public building and place.
<v Joe Lipari>一项PL240-60和一项PL490-20。一个是发出恐怖威胁，第二个是虚假报告公共建筑和场所。

00:12:55.570 --> 00:13:51.180
<v Joe Lipari>When the DA gets up-- or the assistant DA, whatever you call them-- gets up and reads, in front of the judge, reads the charges and then reads the quote aloud in the court room-- and you could just feel everyone in the court room sink. Like you could hear a pin drop. In retrospect, it sounds bad. And the judge even looked at me with a weird look. And I start giggling. When it gets to the AR-10, I giggle. The ADA was like, the people understand that Mr. Lipari is a comedian and what he said is for the benefit of his friend. And so, right there, I'm like, so why are we even here if you understand it? If you understand I'm just a schmuck that doesn't know when to keep his mouth shut, why are we here?
<v Joe Lipari>当地方检察官走上台——或者助理地方检察官，随便你怎么称呼他们——走上台，在法官面前宣读指控，然后大声在法庭上宣读那段引文时——你能感觉到法庭上的每个人都脸色一沉。那种感觉安静得连根针掉在地上都能听见。回想起来，这听起来确实很糟糕。法官甚至用一种怪异的眼神看着我。我开始咯咯笑。当读到AR-10时，我咯咯笑了。助理地方检察官说，公众理解利帕里先生是一名喜剧演员，他所说的话是为了博取朋友一笑。所以，就在那一刻，我想，既然你们理解，那我们为什么还要在这里？如果你们理解我只是一个管不住自己嘴的笨蛋，我们为什么会在这里？

00:13:51.180 --> 00:14:36.180
<v Ben Calhoun>Since then, Joe's been to court more than half a dozen times over this. And from the sound of it, he hasn't been making things any easier for himself. By his own account, he's given one or two speeches about the Constitution and about how the terrorists have won. Not exactly one to dress for court, Joe recently got scolded for wearing Hawaiian shorts in the courtroom. Joe pointed to the guy next to him and said, that guy's wearing sweatpants. This brings us to what might actually be the most remarkable thing about Joe's whole story, which is this. Most of us learn at some point in our lives that we shouldn't always say whatever impulsive thing we feel like saying. And if we didn't learn that lesson, by getting in trouble in school or making someone cry, the day the cops showed up at our door with machine guns, or the day we had to stand up in court and face felony terrorism charges over a broken cell phone-- those things would be a red light, where we would stop and reevaluate. But has Joe reevaluated, considered whether his life would be easier if he changed?
<v Ben Calhoun>从那以后，乔为了这事已经上过半打多次法庭了。听上去，他并没有让自己过得更轻松。按他自己的说法，他已经发表了一两次关于宪法以及恐怖分子如何获胜的演讲。乔可不是那种会为了上庭而特意打扮的人，他最近因为在法庭上穿夏威夷短裤而挨了训。乔指着旁边的一个家伙说，那家伙穿着运动裤呢。这就把我们带到了乔整个故事中最引人注目的一点，那就是：我们大多数人在生活中的某个时刻都会明白，我们不应该总是说任何我们想说的冲动话。如果我们没有从在学校惹麻烦或惹哭别人中吸取教训，那么当警察带着机关枪出现在我们门口的那一天，或者当我们不得不站在法庭上面临因一部坏手机而引发的重罪恐怖主义指控的那一天——这些事情本该是一盏红灯，让我们停下来重新评估。但是乔有没有重新评估过，有没有考虑过如果他改变一下，他的生活会不会更轻松？

00:14:52.070 --> 00:15:19.030
<v Joe Lipari>No. I don't think so. I haven't yet. No. Yeah, I don't think I've ever gotten to a point where I'm like, oh man, I'm going to change who I am. No, absolutely not. I can't. I don't know how. Even if I wanted to shut my mouth more often, I don't think I have the facilities to do so.
<v Joe Lipari>没有。我不这么觉得。我还没呢。没有。是的，我不认为我曾经达到过那种境界，觉得，哦天哪，我要改变我是谁。不，绝对没有。我不能。我不知道怎么变。即使我想管住自己的嘴，我也觉得我没有那个能力。

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:22.490
<v Ben Calhoun>Why not?
<v Ben Calhoun>为什么不呢？

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:45.420
<v Joe Lipari>I don't know. I can't. I don't want to. I don't want to-- because some of the people who love me, love me for that. The people who hate me, hate me for that. So why am I going to worry about the people who hate me and not the people who love me?
<v Joe Lipari>我不知道。我不能。我不想。我不想——因为有些爱我的人，就是因为这个才爱我。恨我的人，也是因为这个才恨我。那我为什么要为那些恨我的人操心，而不是为那些爱我的人操心呢？

00:15:45.420 --> 00:16:28.880
<v Ben Calhoun>In the last few months, the District Attorney's office offered Joe a number of plea deals. Some of them were pretty good. Most recently, a misdemeanor with some community service. Joe rejected all of them. He wanted a jury trial. He was sure no jury would convict him. Finally, Joe went back to court again. And it seems he kind of won. He's got what's called an adjournment and contemplation of dismissal, which essentially means his case is on ice for the next six months. And then, the prosecutors will drop the charges against him. There is, of course, one hitch to all that, one thing that could ruin this and put Joe right back on the hook. During the next six months, Joe's got to stay out of trouble.
<v Ben Calhoun>在过去几个月里，地区检察官办公室向乔提出了多项认罪协商方案。其中有些方案相当不错。最近的一次是一项轻罪指控，外加一些社区服务。乔拒绝了所有这些方案。他想要由陪审团审判。他确信没有任何陪审团会判定他有罪。最终，乔再次回到法院。而且看起来他某种程度上赢了。他得到了所谓的延期审理并考虑撤销起诉，这基本上意味着他的案子在接下来六个月里被搁置。然后，检察官将撤销对他的指控。当然，这一切有一个阻碍，有一件事可能会毁掉这一切，让乔再次陷入困境。在接下来的六个月里，乔必须惹事生非。

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:38.650
<v Ben Calhoun>[MUSIC - "RIGHT TO REMAIN SILENT" BY THE SILVER JEWS]
<v Ben Calhoun>[音乐 - 银色犹太乐队的《保持沉默的权利》]

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:55.650
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, another guy who's got no problem standing up to the police though in his case it's because he is the police. That's in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>本·卡尔霍恩是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，将是另一个人，他毫不畏惧与警察对抗，不过在这个案例中，是因为他自己就是警察。稍后播出。来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共国际广播电台，我们的节目稍后继续。

00:16:55.650 --> 00:17:35.950
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Our show today-- Right to Remain Silent. We have two stories of people who very much do not choose to remain silent. We've arrived at act two of our show. Act Two. Is That a Tape Recorder in Your Pocket, or Are You Just Unhappy to See Me? Adrian Schoolcraft is a New York City policeman who decided to secretly record himself and his fellow officers on the job-- all day, every workday, he says for 17 months. Including lots of days when he was ordered to do all kinds of things cops are not supposed to do. It's led to a small scandal, Several people removed from their jobs, and four investigations of the New York Police Department. Though Adrian insists he didn't get into this looking for trouble.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目——《保持沉默的权利》。我们有两个关于坚决不选择保持沉默的人的故事。我们来到了节目的第二幕。第二幕。你口袋里那是录音机，还是你很高兴见到我？阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特是一名纽约市警察，他决定在工作中秘密录下自己和同事的对话——每天，每个工作日，他说持续了17个月。其中包括许多天，他被命令去做各种警察不应该做的事。这引发了一场小型丑闻，几人被撤职，并引发了对纽约警察局的四项调查。虽然阿德里安坚称他卷入此事并非为了找麻烦。

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:42.520
<v Graham Rayman>His father is a police officer, and, I would say, he went along with the program for a few years.
<v Graham Rayman>他的父亲是一名警官，而且，可以说，他有几年是顺从体制的。

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:54.800
<v Ira Glass>This is the reporter who broke the story in The Village Voice about Adrian and what he recorded those 17 months, a reporter named Graham Rayman. When I asked Graham what Adrian, the person at the center of this scandal, is like, the first thing out of his mouth is--
<v Ira Glass>这位是《乡村之声报》的记者，他独家报道了阿德里安以及他在那17个月里录下内容的内幕，这位记者名叫格雷厄姆·雷曼。当我问格雷厄姆，处于这场丑闻中心的阿德里安是个什么样的人时，他脱口而出的第一句话是——

00:17:54.800 --> 00:18:11.890
<v Graham Rayman>I would describe him as an extremely earnest person, almost-- in this cynical age-- almost to the point of almost too earnest. He actually believed that he could get the police commissioner to change certain things about how the police department was being run.
<v Graham Rayman>我会形容他是一个极其认真的人，几乎——在这个愤世嫉俗的时代——几乎到了几乎过分认真的地步。他真的相信自己能让警察局长改变警察局运作方式的某些方面。

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:38.020
<v Ira Glass>Adrian Schoolcraft was working in Brooklyn-- precinct 81, in Bedford-Stuyvesant, a rough neighborhood, mostly black, that was slowly gentrifying. The precinct is just seven blocks wide and 20 blocks long, roughly, and had 13 murders last year, which is a third of what it used to be. Adrian's kind of an electronics buff, and he bought himself one of those tiny digital recorders, tucked it in his breast pocket, and started recording-- as he walked his beat, when he talked to other cops--
<v Ira Glass>阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特在布鲁克林工作——第81分局，位于贝德福德-斯图维森特，这是一个治安复杂的社区，以黑人为主，正在缓慢进行中产阶级化。该分局大约只有七个街区宽、二十个街区长，去年发生了13起谋杀案，是过去的三分之一。阿德里安有点电子产品迷，他给自己买了一个微型数码录音机，把它塞进胸前的口袋里，然后开始录音——当他在巡逻时，当他与其他警察交谈时——

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:39.330
<v Police Sergeant>All right, attention. Roll call.
<v Police Sergeant>好了，注意。点名。

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:40.840
<v Ira Glass>--morning roll calls.
<v Ira Glass>——早晨点名。

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:41.640
<v Police Sergeant>Enison.
<v Police Sergeant>埃尼森。

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:42.300
<v Enison>Here.
<v Enison>到。

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:43.280
<v Police Sergeant>Lewis.
<v Police Sergeant>刘易斯。

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:48.670
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>The only reason the thought entered my head was because-- to protect myself.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>我脑子里冒出这个想法的唯一原因是因为——为了保护我自己。

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:49.900
<v Ira Glass>This is Adrian.
<v Ira Glass>这是阿德里安。

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:59.760
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Like any other officer would carry a recorder-- was to protect themselves from any false accusations. Usually from civilians who are upset.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>就像任何其他警官会随身携带录音机一样——是为了保护自己免受任何虚假指控。通常来自不滿的平民。

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:01.680
<v Ira Glass>How big was the recorder?
<v Ira Glass>录音机有多大？

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.850
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Oh, about the size of a pack of gum.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>哦，大概是一包口香糖那么大。

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:18.860
<v Ira Glass>The atmosphere at the 81st precinct was set by its commander, Stephen Mauriello. When Mauriello showed up, Adrian Schoolcraft says, things changed. Offices were told to write more tickets, do more stop-and-frisks, arrest more people for low level offenses that they might otherwise let go-- get their numbers up.
<v Ira Glass>第81分局的氛围是由其指挥官斯蒂芬·毛里埃洛确定的。阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特说，当毛里埃洛出现时，情况变了。警察被告知要开出更多罚单，进行更多盘查搜身，以低级别违法行为逮捕更多人，而这些行为平时他们可能会放过——提高他们的指标。

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:31.610
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>The pressure definitely increased when he arrived and took over as the commanding officer. The analogy I would uses is like having a boot to the back of your heel. It is do this or else. The rent's due.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>当他到来并接任指挥官后，压力明显增加了。我会用的比喻就像是有一只靴子踩在你的后脚跟上。要么照做，要么滚蛋。房租到期了。

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:32.560
<v Ira Glass>The rent's due?
<v Ira Glass>房租到期了？

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:35.210
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>The rent is due. Pay the rent. Did you pay the rent last month?
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>房租到期了。交租金。你上个月交房租了吗？

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:37.090
<v Ira Glass>Pay the rent means did you get your numbers?
<v Ira Glass>交房租的意思是你达到指标了吗？

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:38.160
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Correct.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>正确。

00:19:38.160 --> 00:20:02.640
<v Ira Glass>Now, it's perfectly legal for police to be told-- like anybody in any job-- here's the amount of work that we expect you to do, number of tickets and arrests that are normal for somebody in your job in this neighborhood. But what's not allowed is to penalize police officers who do not make those targets. We don't want police officers under such pressure to deliver numbers that they make stops and arrests and write summons with no valid reason, just to get their goals. Again, reporter Graham Rayman.
<v Ira Glass>现在，警察被告知——就像任何工作中的任何人一样——这就是我们期望你完成的工作量，在这个社区中做你这份工作的人的正常罚单和逮捕数量，这完全是合法的。但不允许的是惩罚那些未能达到这些目标的警察。我们不希望警察在如此大的指标压力下，在没有正当理由的情况下进行拦截、逮捕和开传票，仅仅为了达到他们的目标。再次请出记者格雷厄姆·雷曼。

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:13.830
<v Graham Rayman>In other words, as a police supervisor I can't tell you, you better give me 20 tickets a month or else I'm going to transfer you to the graveyard shift. There can't be a direct relationship between the two.
<v Graham Rayman>换句话说，作为一名警察主管，我不能告诉你，你最好在一个月内给我开20张罚单，否则我要把你调到夜班。这两者之间不能有直接联系。

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:14.730
<v Ira Glass>That's just against the rules.
<v Ira Glass>这违反了规则。

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.470
<v Graham Rayman>It's against the law.
<v Graham Rayman>这是违法的。

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:17.480
<v Ira Glass>Oh, it's against the law?
<v Ira Glass>哦，这是违法的？

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:36.770
<v Graham Rayman>Yeah, there's a state law against that kind of thing. But what was happening in the precinct, and what the tapes show repeatedly, is that they were tying it to disciplinary action. They were threatening the cops. If you don't hit your numbers, you'll get transferred, you'll lose your assignment, we'll change your partner, you'll go on a foot post, you can be given a worse assignment.
<v Graham Rayman>是的，有一项州法律反对这种事情。但分局里发生的事情，以及录音反复显示的，是他们将指标与纪律处分挂钩。他们在威胁警察。如果你达不到指标，你就会被调职，你会失去分配的岗位，我们会给你换搭档，你会去步行巡逻岗，你会被分配到更糟的岗位。

00:20:36.770 --> 00:20:55.450
<v Ira Glass>On November 1, 2008, one sergeant declares at a roll call, quote "they are looking at these numbers and people are going to be moved. They can make your job real uncomfortable, and we all know what that means." On December 8, 2008, the sergeant tells the officers that if they don't get their activity up, quote, "there's some people here that may not be here come next month."
<v Ira Glass>2008年11月1日，一名警长在一次点名中宣称：“他们正在查看这些数字，有人要被调动了。他们能让你的工作非常难受，我们都知道这意味着什么。”2008年12月8日，该警长告诉警员们，如果他们不提高工作量，“这里有些人到下个月可能就不在这里了。”

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:58.960
<v Police Sergeant>There's some people here that may not be here come next month.
<v Police Sergeant>这里有些人到下个月可能就不在这里了。

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:13.750
<v Ira Glass>Because officially the NYPD doesn't allow numeric quotas to be tied to job performance, you hear the supervisors in the recording sometimes get into real verbal contortions to get the point across. Like in this excerpt from a roll call the first month that Schoolcraft was recording, June 2008.
<v Ira Glass>由于官方规定纽约警察局不允许将数字配额与工作表现挂钩，有时你会听到录音中的主管们为了把意思表达清楚，使用了真正的语言扭曲。就像斯库尔克拉夫特开始录音的第一个月（2008年6月）的一次点名摘录。

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:18.460
<v Police Sergeant>The XO was in the other day. I don't know who was here. He actually laid down a number.
<v Police Sergeant>行政长官前几天来过了。我不知道谁在这里。他确实定了一个数字。

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:23.700
<v Ira Glass>I'm just going to repeat this because it's hard to hear. "The XO was in the other day," that's a commanding officer, right?
<v Ira Glass>我只是要重复一遍，因为很难听清。“行政长官前几天来过了，”那是个指挥官，对吧？

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:24.200
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>The Execuitve Officer.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>行政长官。

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:27.060
<v Ira Glass>Or, the Executive Officer. --"was in the other day. He laid down a number."
<v Ira Glass>或者，行政长官。“——前几天来过了。他定了一个数字。”

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:31.490
<v Police Sergeant>All right. So, I'm not going to quote him on that, because I don't want to be quoted stating numbers.
