00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:07.570
<v Ira Glass>I don't know what the number is, but that's what he wants. That's a really-- what does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>我不知道具体数字是多少，但这就是他想要的。这真的很——那是什么意思？

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:20.870
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>He's playing the same game. He knows he's not supposed to state a number, but he wants to get his point across. So it's kind of like, if you remember All the President's Men , it's a non-denial denial.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他在玩同样的游戏。他知道自己不应该说明具体数字，但他想把意思表达清楚。所以有点像，如果你还记得《总统班底》，那是一种非否认式的否认。

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:58.520
<v Ira Glass>Adrian Schoolcraft says he isn't exactly sure when, but at some point he had decided that it was important to document the orders that he was given that he thought were out of line. He recorded roll calls where officers were constantly being told to do more stop-and-frisks, even though it's illegal to stop a random person on the street and frisk them without reasonable suspicion. In December 2008, a sergeant tells officers to stop-and-frisk quote, "anybody walking around, no matter what the explanation is." He recorded Stephen Mauriello, the commander the 81st precinct-- and the person Adrian Schoolcraft says really brought the hammer down for higher numbers-- ordering the officers to arrest everyone they see. This happens in a couple of recordings, like this one from Halloween 2008.
<v Ira Glass>阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特说他不太确定具体是什么时候，但在某个时刻他决定，记录下他被下达的那些他认为越界的命令是很重要的。他录下了点名过程，会上警察们不断被告知要进行更多的盘查搜身，尽管在街上随机拦截一个人并在没有合理怀疑的情况下搜身是违法的。2008年12月，一名警长告诉警察要盘查搜身，“任何在街上走动的人，无论有什么解释。”他录下了斯蒂芬·毛里埃洛的声音——第81分局的指挥官，也是阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特所说真正下狠手逼迫提高指标的人——他命令警察逮捕他们看到的每个人。这在几次录音中都有发生，比如2008年万圣节的这一次。

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:03.800
<v Stephen Mauriello>Any roving bands-- you hear me-- roving bands more than two or three people--
<v Stephen Mauriello>任何游荡的团伙——你们听着——超过两三个人的游荡团伙——

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:09.410
<v Ira Glass>He's saying "any roving bands of more than two or three people"-- he's talking about just people going around on Halloween night--
<v Ira Glass>他说的是“任何超过两三个人的游荡团伙”——他说的是万圣节晚上在外面闲逛的人——

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:10.450
<v Stephen Mauriello>I want them stopped--
<v Stephen Mauriello>我要他们被拦截——

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:11.610
<v Ira Glass>I want them stopped--
<v Ira Glass>我要他们被拦截——

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:12.400
<v Stephen Mauriello>--cuffed--
<v Stephen Mauriello>——戴上手铐——

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:13.850
<v Ira Glass>--cuffed--
<v Ira Glass>——戴上手铐——

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:15.330
<v Stephen Mauriello>--throw them in here, run some warrants.
<v Stephen Mauriello>——把他们扔进这里，查查有没有未结Warrant（搜查令/拘传令）。

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:17.040
<v Ira Glass>--throw them in here, run some warrants.
<v Ira Glass>——把他们扔进这里，查查有没有未结Warrant。

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:21.420
<v Stephen Mauriello>You're on a foot post? [BLEEP] it. Take the first guy you've got and lock them all up. Boom.
<v Stephen Mauriello>你在步行巡逻岗？操他的。抓你碰到的第一个家伙，把他们全锁起来。砰。

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:25.520
<v Ira Glass>You're on a foot post? F it. Take the first guy you've got, lock them all up. Boom.
<v Ira Glass>你在步行巡逻岗？操它。抓你碰到的第一个家伙，把他们全锁起来。砰。

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:28.810
<v Stephen Mauriello>We're going to go back out and process them later on, I've got no problems--
<v Stephen Mauriello>我们要回去稍后处理他们，我没问题——

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:32.770
<v Ira Glass>--go back out and then we'll come back in and process them later on."
<v Ira Glass>——再出去，然后我们回来稍后处理他们。”

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:38.560
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Yes. Yeah, what he's saying is, arrest people simply for the purpose of clearing the streets.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>是的。是的，他的意思是，逮捕人纯粹是为了清空街道。

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:41.460
<v Ira Glass>Again, Graham Rayman. He says the problem with that is--
<v Ira Glass>再次请出格雷厄姆·雷曼。他说这样做的问题在于——

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:48.720
<v Graham Rayman>There has to be a violation of the law to make an arrest. He's essentially making the arrest before the crime takes place.
<v Graham Rayman>必须有违法行为才能实施逮捕。他本质上是在犯罪发生之前就进行逮捕。

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:57.190
<v John Eterno>This is an example of something that I would say-- they're going out in the street and just grabbing people-- that's unlawful imprisonment. It's an illegal arrest.
<v John Eterno>这是一个我要说的例子——他们走到街上随便抓人——那是非法拘禁。这是一次非法的逮捕。

00:23:57.190 --> 00:24:27.230
<v Ira Glass>That's John Eterno, a former New York City cop, who went up the ranks from officer to sergeant, to lieutenant, to captain. He now chairs the Department of Criminal Justice at Molloy College and researches and writes about police practices with Professor Eli Silverman. And he says that some of the things that Adrian Schoolcraft documented on his recordings were no surprise to anybody-- like sergeants hounding officers to get their numbers up. That's been happening in every precinct for a long time, he says. But for commanders to tell cops, just lock people up and figure it out later-- Eterno says the word for that is kidnapping.
<v Ira Glass>那是约翰·埃特诺（John Eterno），前纽约市警察，从警员晋升为警长、中尉，再到上尉。他现在担任莫莫洛伊学院刑事司法系主任，并与伊利·西尔弗曼教授一同研究和撰写有关警察实务的文章。他说，阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特在他的录音中记录的一些事情对任何人来说都不足为奇——比如警长死缠烂打地逼迫警察提高指标。他说，这种情况在每个分局已经发生很长时间了。但是对于指挥官告诉警察，先把人锁起来再弄清楚情况——埃特诺说，这个词叫做绑架。

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:49.740
<v John Eterno>That's exactly what it is. They're just pulling people off the street. It's an unlawful imprisonment and they're being kidnapped. If they don't have probable cause, you cannot grab people off the street. It is kidnapping. At this point, from what I'm hearing on this tape, it seems to me that this is probably illegal behavior that's taking place on the part of the police department.
<v John Eterno>事实正是如此。他们只是把人从街上强行拖走。这是非法拘禁，他们被绑架了。如果他们没有合理根据，你不能把人从街上抓走。这就是绑架。此时此刻，从我在这段录音中听到的内容来看，在我看来，这很可能是警察局内部正在发生的违法行为。

00:24:49.740 --> 00:25:06.630
<v Andre Wade>We were arrested, they take us to the 81st precinct, put us in lock up for maybe an hour or two. And they processed us and checked for warrants. And once they see no warrants, they let us go, but we were still issued a citation.
<v Andre Wade>我们被捕了，他们把我们带到第81分局，把我们关在拘留所里大概一两个小时。他们给我们做了登记并检查了搜查令。一旦他们发现没有搜查令，他们就放了我们，但我们还是收到了一张传票。

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:23.190
<v Ira Glass>Andre Wade has lived in the neighborhood for over twenty years. He's a commercial driver. One day, he and two friends were picking up his brother to go to work together. They were standing on the sidewalk, and a police officer came over, and said they were trespassing. When his brother came down and confirmed, no, no they were there to pick them up, Andre says the officer wouldn't listen.
<v Ira Glass>安德雷·韦德在这个社区住了二十多年。他是一名商业司机。一天，他和两个朋友去接他弟弟一起去上班。他们站在人行道上，一个警察走过来，说他们私闯民宅。当他弟弟下楼并确认，不，不，他们是来接他们的时候，安德雷说警察根本不听。

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:59.480
<v Andre Wade>He was just saying stuff like, you know you're not supposed to be standing here. He started getting upset when we were trying to talk him out of giving us the citations. And it's like he just got out of control. He got real erratic and got on the radio. And the next thing you know, we turn around and there's eight, nine police cars. It was to the point to where you would think that somebody was getting arrested for murder, or something like that. And they were just jumping out of their vehicles, and me and my buddies already knew that we were in for a ride.
<v Andre Wade>他只是一直说一些诸如，你知道你不应该站在这里之类的话。当我们试图说服他不要给我们开传票时，他开始生气了。感觉他简直失控了。他变得非常反常，并开始用对讲机呼叫。紧接着你一转身，就有八、九辆警车赶到。那种阵势，你会以为有人因谋杀或其他什么罪名被捕了。他们从车里跳出来，我和我的哥们儿心里已经清楚，我们要倒霉了。

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:07.040
<v Ira Glass>The citation that the police gave Wade lists his name, the day that he's supposed to appear in court, but in the spot where it's supposed to specify his crime--
<v Ira Glass>警察给韦德的传票上列出了他的名字，他应该出庭的日子，但在应该明确他罪行的空白处——

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:15.330
<v Andre Wade>Yes, in that field of the ticket there was nothing-- no violation. The violation was blank.
<v Andre Wade>是的，罚单的那个栏目里什么都没有——没有违法行为。违法行为那一栏是空白的。

00:26:15.330 --> 00:27:06.370
<v Ira Glass>One of the producers of our radio show lives in the 81st precinct. And she says that it's one of those neighborhoods where everybody has stories of ridiculous tickets. One of her neighbors was bringing his aunt home from the hospital, and he double parked. Two officers told him to move his car, and when he didn't, he was handcuffed, forced to lie down in the street, and tasered twice-- all in front of a crowd of people, including her, who live on the block and heard him calling for help. One common citation is for having an open container of alcohol. One neighbor says he was walking home from church with his six year old daughter, drinking a small carton of Tropicana orange juice, and he got a ticket for that. Others got tickets for water and Gatorade that was being given away at the park. George Walker has lived on the same block for over 40 years and says older guys like him get a lot of tickets. He thinks maybe they're targeted because they don't give the cops any fuss. He says he's gotten a dozen tickets this past year, nearly all for open container, even though he says he wasn't drinking alcohol.
<v Ira Glass>我们广播节目的一位制作人住在第81分局辖区。她说这是那种每个人都有荒唐罚单故事的社区。她的一个邻居正带着他阿姨从医院回家，他双排停车。两名警察叫他把车挪开，当他没有挪时，他被戴上手铐，强行按倒在街上，并被泰瑟枪电击了两次——这一切都发生在周围一大群人的注视下，包括她，他们住在那个街区，听到了他呼救的声音。一张常见的罚单是关于携带开封的酒精饮料。一位邻居说他正带着六岁的女儿从教堂步行回家，喝着一小盒纯果乐橙汁，他因此吃了一张罚单。其他人则因为公园里免费发放的水和佳得乐而吃了罚单。乔治·沃克在同一个街区住了40多年，他说像他这样的大龄男子经常吃罚单。他认为他们可能是被盯上了，因为他们不会给警察找麻烦。他说他在过去的一年里收到了十几张罚单，几乎全部都是因为开封的容器，尽管他说他并没有喝酒。

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:38.690
<v George Walker>Every last ticket was dismissed. Every one was not a valid ticket. Because if you see someone drinking alcohol, and you give them a ticket for open container, you have to name what they were drinking. But if they can't name it, they just say cup with alcohol in it. But that's not the name of the alcohol, so it gets dismissed-- because it wasn't alcohol in the first place. But they feel like they can do anything the want to us.
<v George Walker>每一张罚单最后都被驳回了。每一张都不是有效的罚单。因为如果你看到有人喝酒，你因为开封的容器给他开罚单，你必须说出他们喝的是什么。但如果你说不出名字，你只能说里面有酒精的杯子。但这并不是酒精的名字，所以案子就被驳回了——因为那根本不是酒精。但他们觉得他们可以对我们做任何他们想做的事。

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:50.380
<v Ira Glass>So in this police station, where everybody's obsessed with how many tickets they're writing-- where cops are told to pull people off corners and throw them in jail and figure out later what to charge them with-- comes Adrian Schoolcraft, who had no interest in making his numbers.
<v Ira Glass>因此，在这个人人都在疯狂开罚单的警察局里——在这个警察被要求把路人从街角拉走扔进监狱、然后再琢磨该定什么罪名的警局里——阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特（Adrian Schoolcraft）出现了，他对刷业绩毫无兴趣。

00:27:50.380 --> 00:28:00.850
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No, I never tried to make anything happen. I went out there, and you walk you beat. And whatever happened, happened.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>不，我从来没想过要搞出什么业绩。我出去巡逻，走我的辖区。发生什么就是什么。

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:06.100
<v Ira Glass>When you would talk to other officers in the precinct, did you have friends who felt the same way?
<v Ira Glass>当你和辖区的其他警官交谈时，有没有志同道合的朋友？

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:07.890
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Yes.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>有。

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:10.610
<v Ira Glass>And would they not get the numbers, or would they get the numbers?
<v Ira Glass>那他们是会敷衍业绩，还是会去搞业绩？

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:22.170
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>They would get the numbers. It's easier. Especially if you have a wife, kids. Then they're devoted to their pension and retiring.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他们会去搞业绩。这样比较轻松。特别是如果你有老婆孩子的话。那样的话，他们就会把心思全放在退休金和退休上。

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:24.070
<v Ira Glass>Do you not have a wife and kids?
<v Ira Glass>你没有老婆孩子吗？

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:25.670
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>没有。

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:28.980
<v Ira Glass>And so you wouldn't go up to people just to give them a ticket?
<v Ira Glass>所以你不会为了开罚单而主动去找别人的麻烦？

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:30.130
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>不会。

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:33.540
<v Ira Glass>Because?
<v Ira Glass>为什么？

00:28:33.540 --> 00:29:10.230
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>It just wasn't right. I found I was getting along with a lot of the local business owners, and I started interacting with the residents, and they would tell me who the problems were. Now, if you start messing with the residents, and you start going into the barber shops and writing summonses that I don't feel police officers have any business writing-- they didn't sweep the floor of hair-- these are the same people that could help you perform your job as a patrolman or a police officer. That was my philosophy, and it did work.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>这不对劲。我发现自己和很多当地商户相处得很好，我也开始和居民交流，他们会告诉我谁才是真正的问题人物。听着，如果你开始骚扰居民，跑到理发店里去开一些我认为警察根本没资格开的罚单——比如理发店没扫地上的头发——这些人明明是可以帮你做好巡警或警察工作的人。这就是我的哲学，而且确实管用。

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.740
<v Ira Glass>And so did you get a lot of heat for doing this?
<v Ira Glass>那你这么做有没有挨很多骂？

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:17.590
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>He [UNINTELLIGIBLE] pressure from supervisors.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他[听不清]承受来自上司的压力。

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:18.820
<v Ira Glass>What would they do?
<v Ira Glass>他们会怎么做？

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:30.180
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Well I think they considered the foot post punishment, but I always enjoyed the foot post. But there's also hospitalized prisoners, prisoner transports.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>嗯，我想他们把步行岗当成一种惩罚，但我一直挺喜欢步行岗的。不过还有住院的嫌疑人、押解犯人这类的差事。

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:33.500
<v Ira Glass>So they would assign you to these lousy posts?
<v Ira Glass>所以他们会把你分派到这些讨厌的岗位上？

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:46.050
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Yeah. To get my mind right, they would try those, but I accepted those as normal duties as a police officer.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>对。为了让我“思想端正”，他们会试着这么干，但我把这当成警察的正常职责接受了。

00:29:46.050 --> 00:30:08.620
<v Ira Glass>But we still haven't gotten to the most disturbing thing documented by Adrian Schoolcraft and his recordings. Schoolcraft shows, over and over, that sometimes when real crimes would happen, serious crimes, the 81st precinct would reclassify them as lesser crimes-- or simply not put them in the system at all-- to make it look like the precinct was doing a better job driving down crime rates than it really was. Again reporter Graham Rayman.
<v Ira Glass>但我们还没讲到由阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特及其录音记录下来的最令人不安的事情。斯库尔克拉夫特一次又一次地展示出，有时当真正的犯罪发生、严重的犯罪发生时，第81分局会将它们重新归类为较轻的罪行——或者干脆不录入系统——以此粉饰太平，让外界觉得该分局打击犯罪率的工作比实际做得更好。再次请出记者格雷厄姆·雷曼（Graham Rayman）。

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:25.690
<v Graham Rayman>There's a remarkable conversation that Schoolcraft has with another officer. And the other officer is just telling him three anecdotes of how the precinct commanding supervisor basically dumped three criminal complaints that should've been recorded.
<v Graham Rayman>斯库尔克拉夫特和另一名警官有过一次非同寻常的对话。那名警官向他讲述了三个轶事，讲的是分局指挥官基本上把三个本该记录在案的刑事控告给抹掉了。

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:27.810
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, what are the stories that he tells?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗，他讲的是什么故事？

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:34.340
<v Graham Rayman>One is-- a young woman reports her cell phone was robbed, and the precinct commander basically says--
<v Graham Rayman>一个是——一个年轻女子报警说她的手机被抢了，分局指挥官基本上对她说——

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:35.990
<v Police Officer>--what do you want me to do? What do you want to do with this?
<v Police Officer>——你到底想让我怎么做？你对这个有什么打算？

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:40.830
<v Graham Rayman>What do you want us to do with this? How are we going to solve this? Are you going to get your phone back? You're not going to get your phone back.
<v Graham Rayman>你想要我们怎么处理这事？我们怎么破案？你能把手机找回来吗？你找不回来的。

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:45.100
<v Police Officer>I mean, he's like, "well, what if we can't get it back?" He's like, "are you going to press charges?"
<v Police Officer>我的意思是，他就像是在说，“好吧，要是我们找不回来呢？”他接着说，“你还要起诉吗？”

00:30:45.100 --> 00:31:02.530
<v Graham Rayman>He basically talks her out of filing a complaint, and that should be a robbery that should go in their numbers. And one of the other ones is-- the precinct commander responds to a report of a stolen vehicle. And his first question is, he asks the victim have you done jail time?
<v Graham Rayman>他基本上是劝说她放弃报案，而这本该是一起应计入统计数据的抢劫案。另一个故事是——分局指挥官接报了一起车辆被盗案。他的第一个问题是，他问受害人有没有坐过牢？

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:07.850
<v Police Officer>He's like, "you ever been arrested before?" He's like, "yeah." And he's like, "what for?"
<v Police Officer>他问：“你以前被捕过吗？”对方说：“有。”他接着问：“因为什么？”

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:20.360
<v Graham Rayman>--which is not really a proper question to ask of a crime victim. But he asks it, and the guy says yes. Yeah, I did eight years in prison when I was younger. And the precinct commander says maybe karma stole your car.
<v Graham Rayman>——这其实根本不是该对犯罪受害人问的问题。但他还是问了，那家伙说有。是的，我年轻时在监狱里呆过八年。分局指挥官便说，也许是因果报应偷了你的车。

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:23.520
<v Police Officer>"So you think maybe Karma woke up this morning and took your car?"
<v Police Officer>“所以你觉得可能是因果报应今天早上醒来顺走了你的车？”

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:26.010
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Karma as in the spiritual--
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>因果报应，也就是精神层面的那个——

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:31.220
<v Police Officer>He was like, "no, I don't think Karma takes cars." He's like, "I think somebody took my car."
<v Police Officer>那人说：“不，我不觉得因果报应会偷车。”他说：“我觉得是有人偷了我的车。”

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:33.140
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>So he didn't take his report because he's a felon?
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>所以就因为他有前科，所以就不给他立案了？

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:34.370
<v Police Officer>Yeah. Basically.
<v Police Officer>对。基本上就是这样。

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:40.940
<v Ira Glass>In the end, this cop tells Adrian, their supervisor, Stephen Mauriello, told him to file the case as an unauthorized driver.
<v Ira Glass>最后，这个警察告诉阿德里安，他们的上司史蒂芬·毛里洛（Stephen Mauriello）让他把这个案件作为“未经授权的驾驶人”来归档。

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:45.630
<v Graham Rayman>--meaning that the guy loaned his car to somebody else who now has it.
<v Graham Rayman>——意思是这把车借给了别人，而现在车在别人手里。

00:31:45.630 --> 00:32:25.030
<v Ira Glass>Then when the officer tried to file it that way, because he didn't have a name for the unauthorized driver, he couldn't file it at all. So the robbery went unreported. Rules go into effect in the 81st precinct that make it harder to report serious crimes. Officers are told that if there's a robbery, one of their supervisors has to come out to the scene themselves. And robbery victims are told that if they don't come into the police station, no crime report will be filed at all. After Graham Rayman started publishing these stories about Adrian Schoolcraft, retired cops and some on-duty cops started contacting him with their own anecdotes about crimes being downgraded from serious to much less serious-- the most shocking of these from a high ranking detective name Harold Hernandez.
<v Ira Glass>后来当这名警察试图这样归档时，由于他没有那个“未经授权的驾驶人”的名字，导致根本无法归档。于是这起抢劫案就这样成了未报案件。第81分局开始实施一些新规定，让重大案件的报案变得更加困难。警察被告知，如果发生了抢劫案，必须由其中一名上司亲自赶到现场。抢劫案受害人则被告知，如果他们不亲自去警察局，将根本无法立案。在格雷厄姆·雷曼开始发表这些关于阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特的报道后，退休警察和一些在职警察开始联系他，向他讲述他们自己遭遇的罪案降级内幕——其中最令人震惊的一起，来自一位名叫哈罗德·埃尔南德斯（Harold Hernandez）的高级警探。

00:32:25.030 --> 00:33:11.310
<v Graham Rayman>He's a very distinguished detective. He was working in the 33rd precinct in Washington Heights. And one morning he comes into work and there's a guy who's accused of first degree rape sitting in his interview room. So he sits down and he looks at the guy. And he has a little twinge, and he says, have you ever done this before? And the guy said, yeah. And Hernandez says, how many times? And he says, oh, I don't know, seven or eight. And Hernandez says, where? And he goes, in this neighborhood. And Hernandez is now dumbstruck because there's been no report of a serial rapist-- sexual predator-- working the neighborhood.
<v Graham Rayman>他是一位非常杰出的警探。他在华盛顿高地的第33分局工作。一天早晨，他去上班，发现审讯室里坐着一个被指控犯有一级强奸罪的家伙。于是他坐下来看着那家伙。他心里隐隐觉得有点不对劲，便问：“你以前干过这种事吗？”那家伙说：“干过。”埃尔南德斯问：“多少次？”那家伙说：“哦，我记不清了，七八次吧。”埃尔南德斯问：“在哪里？”那家伙说：“就在这个社区。”埃尔南德斯顿时惊呆了，因为社区里根本没有任何关于连环强奸犯——性掠夺者——出没的报告。

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:15.000
<v Ira Glass>Like, no crimes have shown up. People haven't shown up saying they've been raped or assaulted.
<v Ira Glass>也就是没有任何犯罪记录出现。没有人报案说自己被强奸或侵犯。

00:33:15.000 --> 00:34:00.000
<v Graham Rayman>He hasn't been notified. And he would be notified as a senior detective in the unit. It would be a very big deal. And so he says, can you give me the dates and locations? And the guy says, well, I can try, but you're going to have to take me around and I'll show. I'll show you. So he and a fellow detective get in the car and they drive around. And they look, and the suspect-- whose name is Darryl Thomas-- points out the locations. And then Hernandez takes his notebook and he writes down the locations. And then he goes back and he looks through stacks of crime complaints. And he finds them. And he realizes that they've been classified-- they've been downgraded. They've been classified either as criminal trespassing or criminal possession of a weapon-- both relatively minor crimes, given that the actual conduct in the narrative that the victims are describing is either first degree burglary, robbery, or sexual abuse, sexual assault. And he confronts his bosses about it. He confronts the precinct commander. And he confronts his detective squad commander. And everyone just shrugs. Meanwhile everyone's terrified that it's going to come out-- that these women are going to go to the press, and it's going to be a huge embarrassment, a huge scandal for the department. And if it had come out, it would have been a huge scandal for the department. But the department was able to keep it quiet. The District Attorney's office prosecuted Thomas and he went away for 50 years. But here's the interesting part-- they never publicized the case. There was never a press release issued about it. There was never a news article written about the case.
<v Graham Rayman>他没有接到任何通知。而作为该部门的高级警探，他本该接到通知的。这可是一件大事。于是他说：“你能告诉我日期和地点吗？”那家伙说：“好吧，我可以试试，但你得带我到处转转，我来认。我会指给你看。”于是他和另一位警探上车到处兜风。他们看着，嫌疑人——名叫达里尔·托马斯（Darryl Thomas）——指出了作案地点。接着埃尔南德斯拿出笔记本记下了这些地点。然后他回去翻阅了一叠叠的刑事报案记录。结果他真的找到了。他意识到这些案件被重新归类了——它们被降级了。它们被归类为非法侵入或是非法持有武器——鉴于受害者在陈述中描述的实际行为其实是一级入室盗窃、抢劫或性虐待、性侵犯，这两项都属于相对较轻的罪行。他拿着这些去质问他的老板们。他质问了分局指挥官。他还质问了他的侦缉队队长。然而每个人都只是耸耸肩。与此同时，所有人都害怕事情败露——害怕这些女人会去找媒体曝光，这将会给警局带来巨大的尴尬和惊天丑闻。如果事情真的曝光了，对警局来说确实会是一场惊天丑闻。但警局成功把这事压了下来。地方检察官办公室对托马斯提起了诉讼，他被判入狱50年。但有趣的部分来了——他们从未公开过这个案件。从来没有为此发布过新闻稿。也从来没有关于此案的新闻报道。

00:34:55.490 --> 00:35:13.270
<v Ira Glass>Normally, Graham says, that a case like this-- serial rapist-- they'd try to get some press. But the misclassifications of the crimes would have made the NYPD look bad. No one was ever disciplined for what happened, for downgrading. The precinct commander was promoted twice by Commissioner Kelly.
<v Ira Glass>格雷厄姆说，通常情况下，像这样的案件——连环强奸犯——他们会试图争取一些媒体曝光。但罪案降级会让纽约市警察局颜面扫地。没有人因为发生过这种事、因为降级罪案而受到任何纪律处分。分局指挥官反而在凯利局长手下连升两级。

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:16.100
<v Ira Glass>The guy who was in charge of that precinct where all this stuff happened?
<v Ira Glass>就是那个出了这么多事的辖区的负责人？

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:39.090
<v Graham Rayman>Where this stuff happened. He's been promoted twice. It just went on, business as usual. Hernandez-- here's a guy who probably would've stayed in the department for 35 years, 30, 35-- as long as he could. But he was so upset about this incident and about other instances of downgrading and of manipulation of the crime stats that he retired.
<v Graham Rayman>就是出了这些事的辖区。他已经升了两次职。一切照旧，生意兴隆。而埃尔南德斯——这样一个本可以在警局待上35年、30、35年——只要能干多久就干多久的人。但他对这起事件以及其他降级和操纵犯罪统计数据的行为感到无比气愤，最终选择了退休。

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:43.840
<v Ira Glass>And so the NYPD has denied that crimes were downgraded like this.
<v Ira Glass>所以纽约市警察局否认曾经这样将犯罪降级。

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:56.130
<v Graham Rayman>Yeah. Well, they said that it only happens in a very tiny percentage of cases. And they say that the crimes stats are audited very carefully, And if it was a wider problem it would be spotted.
<v Graham Rayman>是的。嗯，他们说这种情况只发生在极小比例的案件中。他们还说犯罪统计数据经过了非常仔细的审核，如果这是一个更广泛的问题，早就被发现了。

00:35:56.130 --> 00:36:41.130
<v Ira Glass>The New York Police Department declined our request to come onto the radio or to have the officers who supervised Adrian Schoolcraft, and who are heard on his recordings, to be interviewed about their side of all this. But the pressure on police commanders to get better numbers really goes back to 1994, when New York started tracking crimes with a system called CompStat. CompStat, for the first time, gave commanders timely, accurate data once a week on what crimes are happening, so they could send more cops to deal with it. Chances are you've heard of all this. It became one of the best known successes in modern policing. Serious crime has dropped an astonishing 77% in New York City since CompStat began in 1994. Other cities very quickly started imitating it-- DC, Philly, LA. Baltimore's version of CompStat ended up in a recurring plot line on the TV show The Wire , where street cops are told by the bosses to do anything to pump up their numbers. And the problem with CompStat, says Professor Eli Silverman, who studies the way police forces use numbers, is that the early success of CompStat created the expectation that numbers must get better every single year, no matter what.
<v Ira Glass>纽约市警察局拒绝了我们上电台的要求，也拒绝让那些监管阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特、并在录音中听到其声音的警官接受采访，以听取他们对这一切的看法。但警察指挥官们提升业绩数字的压力实际上可以追溯到1994年，当时纽约开始用一套名为CompStat（犯罪统计）的系统来追踪犯罪。CompStat首次让指挥官们每周都能获得及时、准确的犯罪数据，以便他们派遣更多警察去处理。你很可能听说过这一切。它成为了现代警务中最著名的成功案例之一。自从1994年CompStat问世以来，纽约市的严重犯罪惊人地下降了77%。其他城市很快就开始效仿——华盛顿特区、费城、洛杉矶。巴尔的摩版本的CompStat最终成了电视剧《火线》中的常驻情节，剧中的街头警察被上司们要求不择手段地刷高业绩。研究警察部队如何使用数字的伊利·西尔弗曼（Eli Silverman）教授表示，CompStat的问题在于，CompStat早期的成功造成了一种期望，即无论如何，数字必须年年变好。

00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:32.530
<v Eli Silverman>In the beginning it was like an orange. You could squeeze juice from an orange in the beginning much more readily than you can as you extract juice from that orange. And now, it gets harder and harder to drive crime down, because you're compared to not how you were in '94, but how you were last year the same week. And when something's pushed to the excess that it is now, and numbers dominate the system, that's when you have negative consequences.
<v Eli Silverman>一开始它就像个橙子。刚开始时，从橙子里榨汁比你之后从那个橙子榨汁要容易得多。而现在，要把犯罪率压下去变得越来越难，因为和你做对比的不再是94年的情况，而是你去年同一周的情况。当某种东西被推向极端、数字主导了整个系统时，负面后果就会随之而来。

00:37:32.530 --> 00:37:47.350
<v Ira Glass>As apparently the one person in the 81st precinct who was not obsessed by the numbers, Adrian Schoolcraft, by January 2009, had so displeased his bosses that they gave him a failing job evaluation that covered the entire year of 2008-- which meant one thing, Schoolcraft says.
<v Ira Glass>显然，作为第81分局唯一一个不为数字痴狂的人，到2009年1月，阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特已经让他的上司们如此不悦，以至于他们给他打了一个涵盖整个2008年的不及格工作评估——斯库尔克拉夫特说，这意味着一件事。

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:53.320
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>They're starting a paper trail, and they'll just keep documenting. They're starting to move you out.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他们在建立纸质记录，而且会不断记录下去。他们开始把你弄走。

00:37:53.320 --> 00:38:05.970
<v Ira Glass>He hired a lawyer and appealed the evaluation, but started feeling more pressure than ever to go out and do what his bosses wanted. He began to get stomach pains and tightness in his chest. He had trouble sleeping. Again, reporter Graham Rayman.
<v Ira Glass>他请了律师对该评估提出上诉，但却感到了比以往更大的压力，迫使他出去做上司们想要的事。他开始胃痛、胸闷。他开始失眠。再次请出记者格雷厄姆·雷曼。

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:18.030
<v Graham Rayman>I think within the precinct, he was probably seen as a little bit eccentric. And also, he wasn't going with the program. And anyone who doesn't go with the program is automatically marked.
<v Graham Rayman>我想在分局内部，他可能被视为有点古怪。而且，他不愿意同流合污。任何不愿同流合污的人都会自动被盯上。

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:30.570
<v Ira Glass>Schoolcraft began to feel that he was being retaliated against. He got written up for taking a bathroom break without putting it in his log. Another officer was written up for talking to him. When he went to the duty captain, he was told yes, he was being monitored.
<v Ira Glass>斯库尔克拉夫特开始觉得自己正在遭受报复。他因上厕所未在日志中登记而被写了报告。另一名警察因为跟他说话也被写了报告。当他去找值班队长时，对方告诉他确实，他正在被监控。

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:47.750
<v Duty Captain>Because of your past activity. When people at the same level as you and the same post as you, are doing a lot more than what you do when you're out there, we don't know if you're even out there. That's the problem.
<v Duty Captain>因为你过去的行为。当和你同级别、同岗位的人在外面做了远多于你所做的事时，我们甚至不知道你到底有没有在外面。这就是问题所在。

00:38:47.750 --> 00:39:11.670
<v Ira Glass>If there's a bunch of kids on a stoop and you're walking past, the duty captain asks him, and then named some addresses where that might happen, you just go on your merry way, because you don't see anything going on? Schoolcraft tells him he wouldn't just create fake charges. That's a common practice here, he says. Captain asks him what he means, and says in 19 years, he's never seen anybody create charges. Then he asks Schoolcraft the question again.
<v Ira Glass>值班队长问他，如果有一群孩子坐在台阶上，你从他们身边走过——接着队长说出了几个可能会发生这种情况的地址——你是不是就自顾自地走过去了，因为你当做什么都没看到？斯库尔克拉夫特告诉他，他才不会凭空捏造假罪名。他说，这在这里是很普遍的做法。队长问他是什么意思，并称自己在19年里从没见过任何人凭空捏造罪名。接着他又问了斯库尔克拉夫特一遍。

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:15.510
<v Duty Captain>Those kids on the step. Are you going to keep walking?
<v Duty Captain>台阶上的那些孩子。你还要继续走吗？

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:16.460
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>不。

00:39:16.460 --> 00:39:19.420
<v Duty Captain>Are you going to ask them if they live there?
<v Duty Captain>你要问他们是不是住在这里吗？

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:21.920
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>You usually won't get a response, but--
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>你通常不会得到什么回应，但是——

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:26.280
<v Duty Captain>Right. [BLEEP] you, Schoolcraft. Right?
<v Duty Captain>没错。[粗话]去你的，斯库尔克拉夫特。对吧？

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:27.270
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>That's how it usually happens.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>事情通常就是这样的。

00:39:27.270 --> 00:40:14.540
<v Duty Captain>Yeah. Are you going to create something there? Because I could tell you that if that [BLEEP] told me to [BLEEP] myself. Yeah, so you go in the handcuffs for telling me that? Yeah. That's it. If you let that go because there's no violation, because he didn't break the law, then I feel bad for you. Because then you have a tough job. And then maybe you should find something else to do, you know? So if you call that creating something? You call that creating something? Or do you call that a matter of keeping the respect, because they'll step all over you when they see you out there. They'll do whatever they want in front of you when you're out there.
<v Duty Captain>对啊。你打算在那里捏造点什么吗？因为我可以告诉你，如果那个[粗话]让我[粗话]滚蛋的话。对啊，那你因为他对你说了这话就把他拷起来？对啊。就是这样。如果你因为没有违法、因为他没有触犯法律而放过他，那我觉得你挺可怜的。因为那样你的工作会很难做。那也许你该找点别的活儿干，你知道吗？所以你管这叫捏造吗？你管这叫捏造吗？还是你觉得这关乎树立威信，因为当你走到外面时，他们会把你的尊严踩在脚下。当你走到外面时，他们会在你面前为所欲为。

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:23.640
<v Ira Glass>Schoolcraft says that around this time, the recordings became about trying to keep his job. Somebody tells him that one of his bosses wants to force him out on psychiatric grounds.
<v Ira Glass>斯库尔克拉夫特说，大约在这个时候，这些录音的重点变成了如何保住他的工作。有人告诉他，他的某个上司想以精神健康为由逼他卷铺盖走人。

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:28.380
<v Ira Glass>During this whole time that you were recording, who did you tell?
<v Ira Glass>在你录音的整个这段时间里，你告诉过谁？

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:30.870
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>My father knew.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>我父亲知道。

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:32.150
<v Ira Glass>Friends?
<v Ira Glass>朋友呢？

00:40:32.150 --> 00:40:32.840
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>没有。

00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:33.970
<v Ira Glass>Fellow officers?
<v Ira Glass>同行警员呢？

00:40:33.970 --> 00:40:34.940
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>没有。

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:36.520
<v Ira Glass>Were you tempted to tell anybody ever?
<v Ira Glass>你有没有过想告诉任何人的冲动？

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:37.590
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>No.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>没有。

00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:39.660
<v Ira Glass>What'd your dad say?
<v Ira Glass>你父亲是怎么说的？

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:43.080
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>He would ask me if I heard anything that day.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他会问我那天有没有听到什么。

00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:49.730
<v Ira Glass>And when you were getting these orders to get your numbers up and you wouldn't do it, what did your dad say about that?
<v Ira Glass>当你接到这些提高业绩数字的命令却不肯照做时，你父亲是怎么说的？

00:40:49.730 --> 00:40:58.650
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>He would just reiterate to me how the quota system-- wherever you are, whatever city you're in-- it's unethical and it's illegal.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他只会向我重申，配额制度——无论你在哪里，无论你在哪个城市——都是不道德的，也是违法的。

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:00.020
<v Ira Glass>So he was on your side.
<v Ira Glass>所以他是站在你这边的。

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:01.640
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Yes.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>是的。

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:10.030
<v Ira Glass>Finally in April, Schoolcraft takes off a week for stomach and chest pains and is sent to a police department doctor. The doctor finds nothing wrong with him physically.
<v Ira Glass>终于在4月，斯库尔克拉夫特因胃痛和胸痛请假一周，并被派往警察局医生那里。医生发现他的身体没有任何问题。

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:21.280
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>And he asked me if I was experiencing stress or anything. I said, well, yes. Matter of fact, this is what's going on. And he said, are you sure you want to tell me this?
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>他问我是否感受到了压力之类的。我说，嗯，是的。事实上，情况就是这样。然后他说，你确定要把这些告诉我吗？

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:46.130
<v Ira Glass>Schoolcraft says he laid it all out for the doctor-- his bad performance evaluation, the numbers he was asked to hit, and also more personal disputes with his bosses about whether his evaluation was falsified, was the precinct doing training it claimed it was doing. And the police department doctor referred Schoolcraft to see a police department psychologist for an evaluation. And when Schoolcraft tells the psychologist the same things that he told the doctor, she asked him to turn in his gun and shield.
<v Ira Glass>斯库尔克拉夫特说，他把一切都向医生全盘托出——他糟糕的绩效评估、被要求达到的指标，以及与上司之间更具个人性质的争执，比如他的评估是否被篡改、警局是否在进行其声称的培训。警察局的医生随后将斯库尔克拉夫特转诊给警察局的心理学家进行评估。当斯库尔克拉夫特把对医生说的话同样告诉心理学家时，她要求他交出配枪和警徽。

00:41:46.130 --> 00:42:44.410
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Well, she made it sound like it was normal. She said, it's not unusual for us to take an officer's gun and shield if he or she is having chest pains. Schoolcraft moves to a job answering phones at the precinct, where he continues to gather evidence. And in October, he finally talks to the people in the police department who investigate unethical practices-- the Internal Affairs Bureau, IAB-- and it doesn't go well. Schoolcraft says that not only did they seem very skeptical, he claims that Internal Affairs left phone messages for him at the precinct. He says this alerted his bosses to the fact that he was talking to Internal Affairs. Internal Affairs does start an investigation, though. And soon, Schoolcraft gets a phone call from the division of the police department whose main purpose is to make sure that crime reporting and statistics using CompStat are accurate. It's called the Quality Assurance Division. And at last, Schoolcraft says, somebody seems to take his accusations seriously. Investigators hear him out, ask lots of questions, and promise to look into it.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>嗯，她把这说得好像很正常一样。她说，如果警员感到胸痛，我们收回他的枪和警徽，这并不罕见。斯库尔克拉夫特调到警局接听电话的岗位，并在那里继续收集证据。到了10月，他终于与警察局内负责调查不道德行为的部门——内务部（IAB）进行了交谈，但过程并不顺利。斯库尔克拉夫特说，他们不仅显得非常怀疑，他还声称内务部把电话留言留在了警局给他的。他说这让他的上司们警觉起来，知道了他在与内务部接触。不过，内务部确实展开了调查。不久，斯库尔克拉夫特接到了一通电话，来自警察局的一个部门，该部门的主要职责是确保犯罪报告和使用CompStat的统计数据准确无误。这个部门叫做质量保证部。斯库尔克拉夫特说，终于有人似乎认真对待他的指控了。调查人员听取了他的陈述，提出了许多问题，并承诺会彻查此事。

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:48.840
<v Qad Representative>I appreciate you coming in, and bringing [INAUDIBLE] to our attention.
<v Qad Representative>我很感激你能来，并把[听不清]引起我们的注意。

00:42:48.840 --> 00:43:10.380
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't tell them that he has recordings. In fact, as you can hear, he secretly records this three hour meeting with them. But he does give them documentation-- real evidence to back up his charges. And what happens next to Adrian Schoolcraft is very, very strange. Just a few weeks after his meetings with Internal Affairs and QAD, he shows up to work. It's the end of October.
<v Ira Glass>他没有告诉他们自己有录音。事实上，正如你所听到的，他秘密录下了这次与他们长达三个小时的会面。但他确实给他们提供了文件——支持他指控的真实证据。接下来发生在阿德里安·斯库尔克拉夫特身上的事情非常非常诡异。就在他与内务部和质量保证部会面几周后，他去上班了。那是10月底。

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:22.360
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>As soon as I sit down, a lieutenant approaches me and asks for my activity log. Well, this activity log is where I keep a lot of my notes regarding what people are saying and the times they're saying it.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>我一坐下，一名中尉就走近我，索要我的值勤记录。嗯，这个值勤记录是我保存许多笔记的地方，记录着别人说了什么以及他们说话的时间。

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:26.800
<v Ira Glass>And all the things, basically, you're trying to report that you think are going wrong in the precinct.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，也就是你试图报告你认为警局里出错的所有事情。

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:49.190
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Correct. And it wasn't until I got it back that I realized the cat was out of the bag. He had bent the corners on some of the pages, and I saw what piqued his interest. And I became very worried, how he was looming around me-- I felt threatened by it. And again, all these officers are armed. But I left with permission.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>没错。直到我拿回记录，我才意识到事情败露了。他把某些页面的角折了起来，我看到了是什么引起了他的兴趣。我开始非常担心，他一直在我的周围徘徊——我为此感到受到威胁。而且话说回来，这些警官都是配枪的。不过我是在得到允许后离开的。

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:51.190
<v Ira Glass>Because you though, what was going to happen?
<v Ira Glass>因为你当时想，会发生什么？

00:43:51.190 --> 00:43:57.250
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>Well, I wasn't sure. I just felt his behavior worried me. And--
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>嗯，我不太确定。我只是觉得他的行为让我担心。并且——

00:43:57.250 --> 00:44:01.350
<v Ira Glass>But you thought he might provoke you into something, and then he would shoot you, or something?
<v Ira Glass>但你觉得他可能会激怒你，然后他会开枪打你，或者别的什么？

00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:23.620
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>That was one of the fears. I'm not just an officer inside. Now I'm an officer that has this psych issue. No one's supposed to know, but everyone knows that when you have your gun and shield taken, you've been psyched. And you have that brand on you. So what's going to happen? Are they going to say I lunged at him? Or are they-- any kind of scenario could play out. And I just didn't feel comfortable, so I left.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>那是恐惧之一。我不只是内部的一名警员。现在我成了一个有心理问题的警员。本应无人知晓，但每个人都知道，当你的枪和警徽被没收时，你就被当成有精神病了。你身上被打上了那个烙印。所以会发生什么？他们会说我向他扑过去吗？还是他们会——任何剧本都可能上演。我只是觉得很不舒服，所以我离开了。

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:35.510
<v Ira Glass>How he left is in dispute. Schoolcraft says that he told a sergeant that he was feeling sick and went home an hour early. The police say the sergeant never said yes to this request. In any case, Schoolcraft went home and went to bed.
<v Ira Glass>他离开的过程存在争议。斯库尔克拉夫特说，他曾告诉一名中士自己身体不适，并提前一小时回家。警方则表示，中士从未同意这个请求。无论如何，斯库尔克拉夫特回了家并上床睡觉了。

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:50.950
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>A few hours later, I received a phone call from my father, and he told me he received a phone call from my XO. He says, look outside your window. And I looked out my window and there were multiple police vehicles, and there seemed to be quite a crowd.
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>几个小时后，我接到父亲打来的电话，他告诉我他接到了我的行政长官的电话。他说，看看你的窗外。我往窗外一看，有许多警车，而且似乎聚集了不少人。

00:44:50.950 --> 00:44:53.030
<v Police Officer>[KNOCKING] [INAUDIBLE]
<v Police Officer>[敲门声] [听不清]

00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:58.430
<v Ira Glass>Adrian has no idea what they want, but he knows the situation is bad, so he starts recording.
<v Ira Glass>阿德里安完全不知道他们想要什么，但他知道情况不妙，于是他开始录音。

00:44:58.430 --> 00:45:02.310
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>31 October, 2009. [KNOCKING]
<v Adrian Schoolcraft>2009年10月31日。[敲门声]

00:45:02.310 --> 00:45:05.780
<v Ira Glass>The officers open Adrian's door with a key they get from his landlord.
<v Ira Glass>警员们用从房东那里拿到的钥匙打开了阿德里安的门。

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:11.670
<v Police Officer>Adrian! Police department, buddy. Let me see your hands.
<v Police Officer>阿德里安！警察局的，老兄。让我看看你的手。

